# Millbrook is a made-up market town whose places and dates are kept in the town map drawing, so the
# child practises the method here and then uses it on their own place with a grown-up.
lesson history-our-place-long-ago v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=7 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Our place long ago"
  goal "Find out how a place has changed from its maps and its buildings, and look for clues to the past of your own place."

  look {
    scene 36x15 {
      row maps at=canvas(0, 0) space=1 align=start {
        townmap then year=1850 town=Millbrook labels=1
        townmap now year=2026 town=Millbrook labels=1
      }
    }
    say """
      This is Millbrook, a small market town, drawn in 1850 and today. Some places are old: the
      church was built in 1150 and still stands. Some have gone, like the mill. Some are new, like
      the supermarket. Every town has a history like this, and clues to it are everywhere: dates on
      buildings, old names on signs, and old maps in the library.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.town-count k=0
    }
    show history.town-count k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.town-first-built k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.town-standing k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.town-count k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.town-first-built k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.town-standing k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.town-first-built k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    level hard {
      show history.town-standing k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.local-clues
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.how-find-date k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.oldest-building
  }
  remember {
    say "A place keeps clues to its past: old maps, dates on buildings, and names that stay after things have gone."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a local study is done with evidence close to home: old maps, the dates on
    buildings, street names and people's memories. The way in counts the places on Millbrook's map
    in a given year; the core asks which place was built first and how long a place had stood; the
    story is a walk you take together to find three clues; the two-star question asks which source
    would settle a date. The wrong idea to watch for is judging age from looks alone. If the child
    is stuck, ask "how would we check?" The story and the three-star task are for you to mark, on
    a walk and with the oldest building near you; the local library, a museum or the council's
    heritage pages usually have old maps. England's key stage 2 history asks for "a local history
    study". Japan's 社会 for grades three and four begins with 「身近な地域や市（区，町，村）の特色ある地形，土地利用の様子，主な公共施設などの場所と働き」,
    the child's own neighbourhood and town (the course of study as read in its 2008 edition).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example counts the places in 1300, the places compared were built far apart in time, and the years standing are counted to 1850 or 1900 from recent dates."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the maps are of 1950 and today, the places compared were built within a hundred years of each other, the years standing run across centuries, and the two-star question asks which source would settle a disagreement."
  }
}
